BBC BASIC V demonstration programs
==================================

Double-click a program to run it. Most take over the whole screen in their
own screen mode; press Escape (or any key) to stop, then SPACE to get back
to the desktop. WimpClock is a desktop task: it puts an icon on the icon bar.
Shift-double-click a program to load it into Edit and read the listing.

Mandelbrot  The Mandelbrot set drawn point by point in 256 colours (MODE
            28).
Plasma      A 256 colour plasma written straight into screen memory, then
            animated by cycling the palette with COLOUR n,r,g,b.
Fire        The classic fire effect: a heat map averaged upwards, turned
            into colours with ColourTrans and written to screen memory a
            word at a time.
Stars       300 stars flying towards you on a double-buffered screen. Cursor
            Up/Down change the speed.
AsmBars     Raster bars filled by an ARM code routine called with CALL,
            which writes screen memory found with OS_ReadVduVariables.
            Double buffered.
AsmPlot     The inline assembler: a moire pattern drawn by SWI OS_Plot from
            machine code, and stripes written into screen memory.
Cube        A rotating wireframe cube drawn on two screen banks swapped with
            *FX 112 and *FX 113.
Sprites     Shaded balls built pixel by pixel in a sprite area (with masks)
            and plotted with OS_SpriteOp 34 on a double-buffered screen.
Lissajous   Turning Lissajous figures x=SIN(a*t+p), y=SIN(b*t) with ghost
            trails, for eight ratios a:b.
Roses       Twelve rose curves r=COS(k*t) for fractional k, drawn in colour.
Spiral      String art: straight lines in 256 colours make curves.
Circles     Concentric circles, spokes and EOR-plotted ellipses in the 16
            colour MODE 12.
Colours     The 256 colour palette: 64 GCOL colours times 4 tints.
Tree        A fractal tree drawn by a recursive procedure with LOCAL
            variables and GCOL r,g,b.
Life        Conway's Game of Life on an 80 x 56 board. R: random soup, G:
            Gosper glider gun, C: clear.
Hanoi       The Towers of Hanoi with seven discs, solved by a recursive
            procedure and animated on two screen banks.
MouseTest   Shows which mouse buttons a program sees as pressed (MOUSE x,y,b
            and INKEY -10/-11/-12), counts presses, and draws in each
            button's colour while it is held. Handy for checking the
            browser's mouse mapping: Select = 4, Menu = 2, Adjust = 1.
Snake       The game of Snake. Cursor keys (or Z X ' /) steer; eat the
            apples and don't hit the walls or your tail.
Ball        A bouncing ball plotted with exclusive-OR (GCOL 3) and WAIT.
Voices      Plays each of the nine ROM sound voices in turn with VOICE and
            SOUND: WaveSynth, StringLib and Percussion.
Canon       A Pachelbel-style ground bass on six channels, queued a bar at a
            time on the BEATS/TEMPO scheduler with SOUND c,a,p,d,beat.
Tune        A short two channel tune queued on the beat counter with SOUND
            ...,beat, VOICE and STEREO.
WimpClock   A clock written as a Wimp task: an icon bar icon, a window
            redrawn every second from Wimp_PollIdle, and a menu. Only runs
            in the desktop.
Teletext    MODE 7 teletext: colour codes, double height, flashing,
            backgrounds and graphics.
Sieve       The Sieve of Eratosthenes benchmark: 1899 primes, ten times,
            timed.
Guess       Guess the number between 1 and 100, with INPUT.
Errors      ON ERROR, LOCAL ERROR, RESTORE ERROR, ERROR, ERR, ERL and
            REPORT$ at work.

The listings are also in src/basic/demos/ in the source tree.
